---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Exit codes" current="/reference/exit-codes/">
  <article class="doc">
    <header class="doc-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Reference</span>
      <h1>Exit codes</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The bottom line. dollarlint uses small, predictable exit codes so your
        CI can tell the difference between "your config is wrong" and "the
        command itself failed."
      </p>
    </header>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Code</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>0</code></td>
          <td>Clean bill of health — no non-ignored issues found.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>1</code></td>
          <td
            >Something's off — validation issues, parse issues, schema loading
            issues, or compile issues were found.</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>2</code></td>
          <td
            >Can't even get started — the command or configuration couldn't be
            used.</td
          >
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="warning">
      <p>
        If catalog availability is a deal-breaker, use <code
          >--schema-store-failure error</code
        > or <code>schemas.catalogs.failure = "error"</code>. That turns catalog
        load failures into command errors with exit code <code>2</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
